  (u/lit {:yang.call/reason :missing-raise-exit})))))\n  1276\t          public-bridge (u/app (u/lam [escape] activation) u/capture)\n  1277\t          bridged (if tail?\n  1278\t                    (u/if-node\n  1279\t                      (u/app (u/v 'dsg__get) (u/v callee) (u/lit :clj/outcomes?))\n  1280\t                      (u/app (u/v 'yang.call/invoke-with)\n  1281\t                             (u/v callee) (u/v arguments) (u/lit [])\n  1282\t                             (u/lam [condition] condition->outcome)\n  1283\t                             (u/app (u/v 'dsg__assoc) (u/lit {})\n  1284\t                                    (u/lit :clj/outcome-token) (u/v 'dsg__call_token)))\n  1285\t                      public-bridge)\n  1286\t                    public-bridge)\n  1287\t          admitted (u/if-node (u/app (u/v 'dsg__admitted?) (u/v callee))\n  1288\t                              bridged refused)\n  1289\t          ;; A callee carrying a call entry is convention territory whether it\n  1290\t          ;; admits or forges one, so admission rules it. Everything else is a\n  1291\t          ;; data-side invocation - a collection lookup or a raw closure call -\n  1292\t          ;; and the guest library's invoker owns those semantics. The invoker\n  1293\t          ;; is itself a published descriptor, so it is reached the same way\n  1294\t          ;; any guest callee is; its raise escapes to a capture of this site\n  1295\t          ;; and the resumed pass materializes the error outcome here.\n  1296\t          value-call (u/app\n  1297\t                       (u/lam ['dsg__raise_escape]\n  1298\t                              (u/if-node\n  1299\t                                (u/app (u/v 'data/callable?) (u/v 'dsg__raise_escape))\n  1300\t                                (u/let1\n  1301\t                                  (symbol (str reserved-prefix \"invoker_result\"))\n  1302\t                                  (u/app (u/v 'yang.call/invoke)\n  1303\t                                         (u/v 'invoke-value)\n  1304\t                                         (u/app (u/v 'conj) (u/lit []) (u/v callee) (u/v arguments))\n  1305\t                                         (u/lit [])\n    15\t  \"Host module names read by the guest definitions.\"\n    16\t  '#{cell/get data/callable? data/contains? data/count data/map? data/nth\n    17\t     data/string? data/vector?})\n    18\t\n    19\t\n    20\t(def definitions\n    21\t  \"The one ordered definition list used by both emitters.\"\n    22\t  '[;; Descriptor projection remains structural after callable admission.\n    23\t    [yang.call/_descriptor\n    24\t     (fn [callable]\n    25\t       (if (data/map? callable)\n    26\t         (if (= (get callable :type) :cell-ref)\n    27\t           (cell/get callable)\n    28\t           callable)\n    29\t         nil))]\n    30\t    [yang.call/entry\n    31\t     (fn [callable]\n    32\t       (get (yang.call/_descriptor callable) :yang.call/entry))]\n    33\t    [yang.call/eligible?\n    34\t     (fn [callable]\n    35\t       (let [descriptor (yang.call/_descriptor callable)]\n    36\t         (if (nil? (yang.call/entry callable))\n    37\t           false\n    38\t           (if (data/contains? descriptor :yang.call/eligible?)\n    39\t             ((get descriptor :yang.call/eligible?) callable)\n    40\t             true))))]\n    41\t    [yang.call/invocation\n    42\t     (fn [callee positional named raise]\n    43\t       (assoc (assoc (assoc (assoc {} :yang.call/callee callee)\n    44\t                            :yang.call/positional positional)\n    45\t                     :yang.call/named named)\n    46\t              :yang.call/raise raise))]\n    47\t    [yang.call/invocation-with\n    48\t     (fn [callee positional named raise options]\n    49\t       (assoc (assoc (assoc (assoc options :yang.call/callee callee)\n    50\t                            :yang.call/positional positional)\n    51\t                     :yang.call/named named)\n    52\t              :yang.call/raise raise))]\n    53\t    [yang.call/_pair?\n    54\t     (fn [pair]\n    55\t       (if (data/vector? pair)\n   317\t  \"A reference of `kind` (`:stream-ref`, `:cursor-ref`, or `:cell-ref`) to\n   318\t   resource or heap cell `id`, sealed under the task's capability secret.\n   319\t   A task composed\n   320\t   without a secret cannot issue one: it fails closed rather than issue a\n   321\t   reference anyone could forge.\"\n   322\t  [state kind id]\n   323\t  (let [secret (:capability-secret state)]\n   324\t    (when (nil? secret)\n   325\t      (fail (str \"This task has no capability secret, so it cannot issue\"\n   326\t                 \" a resource reference\")\n   327\t            {:reason :no-capability-secret, :kind kind, :id id}))\n   328\t    {:type kind, :id id, :seal (seal-of secret kind id)}))\n   329\t\n   330\t\n   331\t(defn authentic-ref?\n   332\t  \"True when `ref` is a `kind` reference this task issued: its seal\n   333\t   verifies under the task's secret, and it names a live resource of that\n   334\t   kind. A `:cell-ref` is checked against `:heap` instead: its id is live\n   335\t   by `contains?` (a cell may hold nil) and its seal equals the one the\n   336\t   heap entry recorded at allocation, so no hash is computed per access.\"\n   337\t  [state kind ref]\n   338\t  (let [secret (:capability-secret state)\n   339\t        id (when (map? ref) (:id ref))]\n   340\t    (if (= :cell-ref kind)\n   341\t      (let [heap (:heap state)]\n   342\t        (boolean (and (some? secret)\n   343\t                      (= kind (:type ref))\n   344\t                      (contains? heap id)\n   345\t                      (= (:seal ref) (:seal (get heap id))))))\n   346\t      (let [resource (get (:resources state) id)]\n   347\t        (boolean (and (some? secret)\n   348\t                      (= kind (:type ref))\n   349\t                      (some? resource)\n   350\t                      (= (:seal ref) (seal-of secret kind id))\n   351\t                      (= (= :cursor-ref kind) (cursor-cell? resource))))))))\n   352\t\n   353\t\n   354\t(defn check-ref!\n   355\t  \"The resource id a program-supplied `kind` reference names, once its\n   356\t   seal verifies (r10). Anything else fails closed with\n   357\t   `:forged-resource-reference`, naming the effect and the id it named;\n   358\t   a cell ref whose id the heap does not hold is\n   359\t   `:dead-or-forged-reference` instead: its cell was swept, or never was.\n   360\t   Ids are never reused, so such a ref is never silently reallocated.\"\n   361\t  [state effect-kind kind ref]\n   362\t  (if (authentic-ref? state kind ref)\n   363\t    (:id ref)\n   364\t    (let [id (when (map? ref) (:id ref))\n   365\t          dead? (and (= :cell-ref kind) (not (contains? (:heap state) id)))]\n   366\t      (fail (if dead? \"Dead or forged cell reference\" \"Forged resource reference\")\n   367\t            {:reason (if dead?\n   368\t                       :dead-or-forged-reference\n   369\t                       :forged-resource-reference),\n   370\t             :effect effect-kind,\n   371\t             :kind kind,\n   372\t             :id id}))))\n   373\t\n   374\t\n   375\t(declare gensym)\n  1138\tVM-state transitions, with continuation-based control flow (mob D3,\n  1139\tamending the earlier baseline of a threaded immutable heap; cell\n  1140\truling Q1 to Q5):\n  1141\t\n  1142\t- Mutable guest locations are cells. A cell is an ordinary sealed\n  1143\t  reference value `{:type :cell-ref :id :cell-N :seal s}` into a\n  1144\t  task-scoped `:heap` field of the VM value, reached through the `cell`\n  1145\t  host module: `cell/new`, `cell/get`, and `cell/set!`. A write yields a\n  1146\t  new VM value; nothing host-mutable is introduced. No AST tag is added,\n  1147\t  and `yin/def`, Rule R, `:vm/store-put`, and the canonical grammar are\n  1148\t  unchanged.\n  1149\t- Cells have box semantics: they are shared across continuation\n  1150\t  re-entries and never rolled back, so mutations made before a `raise`\n  1151\t  persist at the handler.\n  1152\t- Explicit state threading stays an allowed per-frontend choice. Either\n  1153\t  lowering can be implemented by a stream-consuming interpreter.\n  1154\t- References are logical identities: a `:cell-ref` is data, and a forged\n  1155\t  or foreign ref fails closed at the evaluator.\n  1156\t- Loops lower to recursive lambdas. Continuations are used for the\n  1157\t  escapes (`break`, `continue`, `return`), because a continuation takes\n  1158\t  exactly one argument.\n  1159\t- Abrupt completion (return, throw, break, continue, yield) lowers to\n  1160\t  captured continuations, not explicit completion records (cell ruling,\n  1161\t  owner decision 5). Locals assigned inside `try` bodies and\n  1162\t  continuation-exited loops therefore need cells.\n  1163\t- Suspended state consists of portable code references and data.\n  3553\t#### 8.10.2 Callable representation and identity\n  3554\t\n  3555\tKeep the callable's existing identity and source-specific data. Add the\n  3556\tcommon entry beside its private code and metadata:\n  3557\t\n  3558\t```clojure\n  3559\t;; Illustrative contents of one task-owned Python function cell.\n  3560\t;; `entry` and `body-code` are live Yin closures, not literal AST maps.\n  3561\t{:yang.call/entry entry\n  3562\t :py/type :function\n  3563\t :spec parameter-spec\n  3564\t :defaults defaults\n  3565\t :code body-code}\n  3566\t```\n  3567\t\n  3568\tThe existing `:code` field is not the shared entry: its signature differs\n  3569\tbetween preludes. It may remain private. Cross-language callers must not\n  3570\tinspect it or extract it to bypass the entry.\n  3571\t\n  3572\tA cell-backed callable stores the entry in its cell contents. An\n  3573\timmutable callable descriptor, such as a bound method, may carry it\n  3574\tdirectly in its map. The common prelude projects the entry structurally:\n  3575\tread an existing sealed cell reference through `cell/get`, or inspect\n  3576\tthe immutable descriptor, then obtain `:yang.call/entry`. It must not\n  3577\tdispatch on `:py/type`, `:js/type`, `:php/type`, language names, or a\n  3578\tregistry of languages.\n  3579\t\n  3580\tAn admitted callable is a runtime cell/descriptor constructed or exposed\n  3581\tby the callable constructors or import/export projections of a pinned,\n  3582\tconforming profile. The entry slot is a runtime descriptor field, not a\n  3583\tsource property that promotes arbitrary data to a function. A Python\n  3584\tdictionary, JavaScript object, or PHP array containing a source key\n  3585\tspelled `:yang.call/entry` remains ordinary source data unless an explicit\n  3586\tprofile-defined projection makes it a callable descriptor. Source\n  3587\tproperty access must not overwrite or masquerade as this runtime slot.\n  3588\tThis is an obligation of construction and source resolution, not a new\n  3589\tVM type, descriptor seal, or runtime provenance/language registry. Once\n  3590\tthe resolved descriptor is admitted, the shared projection is structural;\n  3591\tUAST deliberately constructing a conforming descriptor opts into the\n  3592\tconvention rather than changing a source language's data-map semantics.\n  3593\t\n  3594\tMissing entry means not callable. Constructors must install a genuine\n  3595\tunary Yin closure, and supported source mutations must preserve that\n  3596\tinvariant. Unary arity is a constructor/profile conformance obligation,\n  3597\tnot a signature introspection operation: Yin closures are opaque and the\n  3598\tVM's existing argument binding does not enforce this convention's arity.\n  3599\tA key in a map grants no VM privilege; application, ownership, and\n  3600\teffect checks still apply. A non-applicable entry remains an operational\n  3601\tVM application defect, not a source exception caught by the shared helper.\n  3602\t\n  3603\tFor callable kinds whose eligibility can change, the same cell or\n  3604\tdescriptor may also contain `:yang.call/eligible?`, a unary Yin predicate\n  3605\ton the original callable. Without it, an entry is eligible by default.\n  3606\tWith it, the common callable test uses its boolean result. The predicate\n  3607\treads explicit runtime metadata/heap only; it must not mutate state,\n  3608\tevaluate arguments, execute the target body, or invoke getters/user hooks.\n  3609\tIt has the same constructor/arity obligations as the entry. For example,\n  3610\tan instance can keep one dispatch entry while eligibility follows whether\n  3611\tits class currently supplies a supported call method; changing the class\n  3612\tdoes not require rewriting every instance.\n  3613\t\n  3614\tCall eligibility is tested at the callability-check point required by\n  3615\tthe caller's source, not universally before argument evaluation. The\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
