{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_8","type":"agent_message","text":"**REJECT for landing.** The cell-reference fix still mistakes legitimate source maps for references when they contain `:id` and `:seal`.\n\n- **Blocking defect:** [clojure.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-cljrt/src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc:1203) tests only whether those keys exist. For example, invoking `{:type :cell-ref :id :customer :seal \"wax\" :marker 42}` as a map routes it to admission, which calls `cell/get` at [line 2146](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-cljrt/src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc:2146). The VM then rejects the unauthentic reference instead of returning `42`. Key presence is forgeable; authenticity is checked only by the failing dereference. The VM does reject the forged reference, so this finding does **not** establish successful witness bypass, but the routing does not preserve ordinary source-map semantics. The added map regression covers only the case without those keys.\n\nThe other findings from the static review:\n\n- **Globals:** [prelude.cljc](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-cljrt/src/cljc/yang/clojure/prelude.cljc:61) initializes the globals cell once and no longer clears it on re-entry. The composition projects into that cell, and [lower-unit](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-cljrt/src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc:2804) rewrites projected-name reads and caller definitions to the same runtime binding. A caller definition therefore shadows the projection; the former duplicate task-store projection is gone. Persistence across entries is deliberate and documented. The second-entry regression reprojects `answer`, however, so it does not independently prove that an *unprojected* earlier definition survives re-entry.\n- **TypeScript classification:** [js/kind-of](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-cljrt/src/cljc/yang/typescript/antlr/prelude.cljc:117) retains its existing primitive, JS function, and symbol branches, then checks eligibility for other cells. Ordinary JS object cells without a call entry remain objects; no plain-JS classification regression is apparent statically.\n- **Scope:** The five-file diff contains no changes to `yin.vm/**`, `yang/core/call.cljc`, or evidence files. I found no call-node surgery, foreign `:code` extraction, or per-language wrappers. The shape test nevertheless fails the requested admission boundary for legitimate source maps.\n- **Regressions:** The three requested tests assert concrete results: caller shadowing yields `21`, the particular cell-ref-shaped map yields `42n`, and Python function `typeof` yields `\"function\"`. They cover the reported examples, but the map test misses the blocking full-shape case above.\n\nThis was a static review only; I did not run suites. The supplied remote check results remain the dynamic evidence for this commit."}}
